From ab58e28152d7ba21822fce3c47978d6949dc7a72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 13:50:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 087/206] feat(shorebird): the object pool aggregator, and its
 weighted votes

ComputeObjectPoolLinkResults was not their algorithm. It dropped any
op_subgraph_hash_ group holding more than one code object and paired the
survivors directly, which reproduces their 1121/1113/292/16 on the
fixture and is wrong everywhere else.

Their InitializeWithObjectPoolLinkInfo keeps ambiguous groups and votes
on them. Every encoding a `group.size() == 1` test could compile to was
enumerated against the whole 8184-byte function and none is present: no
cmp against #0x60, no iterator bump compared to an end, no asr #5
division followed by cmp #1, and the single cmp #0x1 is a loop-trip
clamp on an index vector rather than a size test.

So this adds ObjectPoolMappingsAggregator. votes_[patch][base] accrues
1.0 / (base_group.size() + patch_group.size()) per pairing, and
GetBestMapping sorts candidates by descending score -- ties going to the
identity mapping -- then takes the first base index not already handed
out. used_ is never cleared, so injectivity holds across the whole link
rather than per call.

The index vector is also conditional, which closes the "self_pp,
subgraph_pp or their union -- cannot discriminate" note in the header.
It is subgraph_pp_indices_ while the base subgraph group is non-empty
and self_pp_indices_ from the first self_hash_ fallback onward, sticky
for the rest of the group because the inner-loop back edge bypasses the
only write of 0.

Three deliberate departures, each in a comment at the site: their inner
loop bound is max(patch.size(), 1) and indexes both vectors, which reads
out of bounds when the base vector is shorter, so this bounds by the
shorter of the two; their fourth groupBy map is built and never read, so
it is not built; their comparator captures the key by reference, which
has no observable consequence.

shorebird_info_check.sh still matches all eight of their report lines,
but that is not evidence: on a fixture where every matched group is 1:1
and every index pair is a bijection, each vote map holds one candidate
and the greedy never rejects, so voting and the old filter agree. The
nine unit tests are what actually pin this, and one of them exists only
because the obvious negative control did not work. Replacing the weight
with a constant left everything green, since a group contributes
|base| * |patch| votes of weight 1/(|base| + |patch|) and score and
event count normally move together. WeightIsNormalisedByGroupSize picks
two groups where they disagree -- 2x2 at 1/4 scores 1.000 against 1x5 at
1/6 scoring 0.833, while the event counts say 4 against 5 -- so dropping
the divisor now flips the answer.

All four negative controls go red and were reverted: constant weight
(1), no injectivity check (2), inverted tie-break (2), unconditional
self_pp (2). verify_all.sh is 6 ran / 0 failed / 137 passed.

Still open: the fixture cannot exercise any of this, so the arbitration
is transcribed rather than behaviourally verified. A synthetic
ObjectPoolLinkInfo pair -- a subgraph group of size 2, and two patch
indices whose best candidate is the same base index -- run through their
gen_snapshot is the only thing that would settle it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

